UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 10, Lesson 1: Combining skills in a mini game', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A mini game combines skills such as running, throwing, catching, bouncing and kicking, and adds rules, space and a purpose. Good mini games have a clear goal, simple rules, a safe boundary, fair teams and a way for everyone to take part. After playing, players review what worked and change a rule to improve the game.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA mini game ends with arguments about who scored. Add one rule or role that makes scoring fair.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAdd a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game fair.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPlan and play a mini game that combines at least three skills from this unit. Write the goal, rules, boundary, scoring and safety rules, then play two rounds. Record one change you made after round one and whether it helped.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"A mini game combines skills such as running, throwing, catching, bouncing and kicking, and adds rules, space and a purpose. Good mini games have a clear goal, simple rules, a safe boundary, fair teams and a way for everyone to take part. After playing, players review what worked and change a rule to improve the game.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-MSP-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Plan and play a mini game that combines at least three skills from this unit. Write the goal, rules, boundary, scoring and safety rules, then play two rounds. Record one change you made after round one and whether it hel Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Add a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game fair. Reasoning: I used a mini game by attending to combining skills rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part. Correction: Add a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a mini game.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a mini game on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a mini game, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Add a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game… This matches the lesson skill for a mini game.","options":["Add a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game…","Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work."],"question":"A mini game ends with arguments about who scored. Add one rule or role that makes scoring fair. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Add a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game…","Careful use of combining skills with clear evidence.","Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part.","A mini game combines skills such as running, throwing, catching, bouncing and kicking, and adds rules, space and a purpose. Good mini games have a clear goal,…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising a mini game?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named combining skills, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the combining skills used."],"question":"A learner is practising a mini game. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Add a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game… uses safe, healthy, respectful choices appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Add a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game…","Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about a mini game?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about a mini game.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about combining skills?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Example mini game ''Collect and deliver'': in a marked 10 metre square, players run to get a soft ball, bounce it twice, pass to a partner…"],"question":"Using this lesson idea — A mini game combines skills such as running, throwing, catching, bouncing and kicking, and adds rules, space… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part.","Plan and play a mini game that combines at least three skills from this unit. Write the goal, rules, boundary, scoring and safety rules, then play…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example and call that independent work."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of a mini game?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a mini game on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Add a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game fair. Reasoning: I used a mini game by attending to combining skills rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with a mini game, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part. Correction: Add a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a mini game.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w10-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a mini game.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Movement Skill and Performance","success_criteria":["I can use a mini game with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify combining skills and explain why it matters.","I can practise a boundary independently on a new prompt.","I can notice changing a rule needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A mini game combines skills such as running, throwing, catching, bouncing and kicking, and adds rules, space and a purpose. Good mini games have a clear goal, simple rules, a safe boundary, fair teams and a way for everyone to take part. After playing, players review what…","options":["A guess with no reason.","A mini game combines skills such as running, throwing, catching, bouncing and kicking, and adds rules, space and a purpose. Good mini games have a clear goal, simple rules, a safe boundary, fair teams and a way for everyone to take part. After playing, players review what worked and change a rule to improve the game.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a mini game?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample mini game ''Collect and deliver'': in a marked 10 metre square, players run to get a soft ball, bounce it twice, pass to a partner who catches it and places it in a hoop. Rules: walk when carrying the ball; wait your turn; 1 point per delivery. After round 1 they widen the hoop to make scoring more even.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA mini game ends with arguments about who scored. Add one rule or role that makes scoring fair.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAdd a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game fair.","explain":"A mini game combines skills such as running, throwing, catching, bouncing and kicking, and adds rules, space and a purpose. Good mini games have a clear goal, simple rules, a safe boundary, fair teams and a way for everyone to take part. After playing, players review what worked and change a rule to improve the game.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A mini game with three skills.","caption":"Skills plus rules.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Skills</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">run, bounce, pass, catch</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Rules</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">walk with ball, wait</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example mini game ''Collect and deliver'': in a marked 10 metre square, players run to get a soft ball, bounce it twice, p Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example mini game ''Collect and deliver'': in a marked 10 metre square, players run to get a soft ball, bounce it twice, p Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a mini game?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample mini game ''Collect and deliver'': in a marked 10 metre square, players run to get a soft ball, bounce it twice, pass to a partner who catches it and places it in a hoop. Rules: walk when carrying the ball; wait your turn; 1 point per delivery. After round 1 they widen the hoop to make scoring more even. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Changing a rule after play.","caption":"Improve one rule.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Round 1</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">arguments about score</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Round 2</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">scorekeeper counts aloud</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a mini game?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part. Here is a more helpful example: Example mini game ''Collect and deliver'': in a marked 10 metre square, players run to get a soft ball, bounce it twice, pass to a partner who catches it and places it in a hoop. Rules: walk when carrying the ball; wait your turn; 1 point per delivery. After round 1 they widen the hoop to make scoring more even.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAdd a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game fair.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a mini game?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA mini game combines skills such as running, throwing, catching, bouncing and kicking, and adds rules, space and a purpose. Good mini games have a clear goal, simple rules, a safe boundary, fair teams and a way for everyone to take part. After playing, players review what worked and change a rule to improve the game. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 10, Lesson 1: Combining skills in a mini game","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"Fundamental movement skills","week":10,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample mini game ''Collect and deliver'': in a marked 10 metre square, players run to get a soft ball, bounce it twice, pass to a partner who catches it and places it in a hoop. Rules: walk when carrying the ball; wait your turn; 1 point per delivery. After round 1 they widen the hoop to make scoring more even.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A mini game with three skills.","caption":"Skills plus rules.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Skills</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">run, bounce, pass, catch</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Rules</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">walk with ball, wait</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Changing a rule after play.","caption":"Improve one rule.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Round 1</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">arguments about score</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Round 2</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">scorekeeper counts aloud</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-pdhpe-y3-w10-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
